   "grading": "Pass requires all MUSTs satisfied: declared types for all parameters and return values; unit identifiers from the declared vocabulary (no free-text strings); machine-readable precision and resolution; machine-readable constraints (ranges, enumerations, nullability); and runtime validation of declared constraints, demonstrated by an invalid input being rejected with a typed error rather than silently coerced, truncated, or accepted. Machine-readable rounding behavior is a SHOULD where the interface format provides a dedicated field for it; its absence does not affect the grade. Partial covers declared types and unit vocabulary present, but one or more of the following not machine-readable: precision or resolution; range constraints; enumeration or nullability constraints. A vendor who meets types and units but exposes constraints only in free-text documentation scores Partial, as does a vendor whose constraints are machine-readable but not enforced at runtime (invalid inputs are silently coerced, truncated, or accepted rather than rejected with a typed error). An interface that applies a declared default without reporting the value used caps at Partial; silent substitution of an undeclared value, with no error and no report, is Fail. Fail covers absent or free-text unit identifiers, types or unit identifiers declared only in documentation rather than in the machine-readable contract, no declared types, or no machine-readable contract at all.",
   "test": "Use the endpoint's introspection mechanism or machine-readable contract to retrieve metadata for at least one unit-bearing input parameter and one unit-bearing response value where the instrument exposes them (a read-only sensor may have no physical-quantity input, and a pure actuator may have no measurement response; check whichever it exposes), and verify the metadata includes declared type, unit identifier from the declared vocabulary, numeric range, precision and resolution and nullability or enumeration constraints; additionally verify rounding behavior is machine-readable where the interface format provides a dedicated field for it. Then submit an input that violates a declared constraint (an out-of-range numeric value or an invalid enumeration member) and verify it is rejected with a typed error rather than silently coerced, truncated, or accepted. Additionally, omit a parameter that carries a declared default and verify the applied value is reported back; submit an unrecognized parameter and verify a typed error rather than a silently substituted value.",
   "rationale": "Autonomous orchestrators generate experiment parameters algorithmically, and silent truncation or undefined behavior on invalid inputs leads to wasted experiments or hardware damage (Abolhasani et al., 2026). Machine-readable declaration does not by itself guarantee enforcement: SiLA 2 and OPC UA LADS standardize the declaration of types, units, and constraints, but neither requires the server to validate those declarations at runtime or to reject a violating input with a typed error, and generated server stubs do not necessarily do so. A fully standards-conformant interface can therefore still be sloppy, declaring constraints and units it does not enforce, which is why enforcement is tested directly rather than assumed; an interface that declares but does not enforce is a remediation target (a Partial), not an automatic disqualification (a Fail). SiLA 2's FDL Unit constraint and OPC UA's EUInformation structure mapped to UNECE unit codes are accepted equivalents under this criterion, but they differ in how much they encode. SiLA 2 defines each unit by decomposition into SI base units (an integer exponent on each base unit, plus a conversion factor and offset), so the dimensional signature and the unit conversions are themselves machine-readable, and the dimensional-compatibility SHOULD does apply to SiLA 2 endpoints. OPC UA's EUInformation, by contrast, references a flat UNECE code list with no dimensional decomposition, so the dimensional-compatibility SHOULD does not apply to endpoints relying on it alone (Juchli, 2022; Hinkel et al., 2023; Brendel et al., 2022)."
