   "requirement": "Full programmatic access MUST be available under commercial and licensing terms that permit autonomous operation; here, \"contract\" means the procurement package, service contract and end-user or API license, not the technical interface contract. Three sub-tests apply. Sub-test (a) No hidden service- or add-on contract paywall. API access required for the buyer's intended autonomous operation MUST be included in the quoted procurement package, with no post-purchase gating of capabilities advertised at the time of purchase. This sub-test targets gating of that baseline autonomous access; it does not prohibit separately priced optional services that leave the baseline intact, such as a regulated-environment support and validation package (GxP, IVD) under which the vendor assumes liability, optional feature updates or upgrades, or a third-party-supplied interface where the vendor itself offers none. Such services may carry a fee, provided the autonomous access included in the quoted package is not itself withheld behind them; per-deployment royalties on that baseline access remain disqualifying. Sub-test (b) License permits ecosystem use. The API license MUST permit publication and reuse of buyer-written integration or adapter code (excluding redistribution of vendor confidential source code, proprietary binaries, or confidential documentation), third-party orchestration, and use without per-deployment royalty. Field-of-use restrictions that prevent reusable buyer-written integration code, restrict multi-tenant deployment, or impose per-deployment royalties are disqualifying. Sub-test (c) Integration documentation included. The procurement package SHOULD include the integration manual and interface documentation at no additional cost, and MUST include them at no additional cost where the interface is not self-descriptive at runtime (criterion C6), since such an interface cannot be integrated without them.",
