   "applicability": null,
   "requirement": "The most basic precondition for workcell integration is that a machine can load labware into the instrument and unload it without manual intervention. The instrument MUST provide an arm-reachable labware handoff position and an API-controlled access path for presenting, accepting, and releasing labware (for example commands to open a door, drawer, or shutter, to extend or retract a carrier or nest and to engage or release a clamp or gripper), or a documented passive handoff in which the arm places directly into a fixed open position. Its geometry MUST be documented for integration: not only the coordinates and approach of the handoff position, but a CAD or 3D model of the instrument itself (a collision model at minimum), so integrators can place it in the workcell and plan collision-free arm trajectories around it. It MUST expose a machine-readable handoff-ready or load-complete indication so the arm knows when it may place or pick labware and when the instrument has secured or released it. The instrument is not required to carry labware identity or the workflow-level history of labware movements; those are maintained by the orchestrator and the LIMS (C5 covers execution-identifier traceability). Device-local observations of labware presence, position, and engagement are reported under C20. These observations may disagree with the orchestrator's planned state after an incomplete or failed handoff, in which case reconciliation, and the decision to continue, recover, or request verification, remains an orchestration responsibility. This criterion is not applicable to instruments that do not receive machine-loaded labware in the workcell (inline pumps, flow cells, fixed environmental sensors and similar).",
