   "grading": "Pass requires a documented arm-reachable handoff position, documented instrument geometry (the handoff position and a CAD or collision model of the instrument), an API-controlled (or documented passive) load and unload path, and a machine-readable handoff-ready or load-complete indication, with no manual step in routine load or unload. Partial covers an automatable mechanism that is API-addressable but missing the handoff-ready or load-complete indication, or with incomplete geometry documentation (the handoff position or an instrument collision model undocumented), or one that requires manual assistance only at setup or commissioning while running unattended thereafter. Fail covers instruments that cannot be loaded or unloaded by a machine without manual intervention during routine operation, since the criterion gates unattended workcell operation. For instruments that do not receive machine-loaded labware, the criterion is graded N/A and the cell-integrated total is computed over the remaining criteria.",
   "test": "Drive the instrument through a complete automated load and unload cycle through the API: open or present the access path, confirm a documented arm-reachable handoff position, place labware (physically or in simulation), command the instrument to accept and secure it, verify the handoff-ready or load-complete indication, then command release and unload. Confirm that routine load and unload require no manual step, and that the instrument's geometry is documented: the handoff position and a CAD or collision model of the instrument for workcell layout and trajectory planning.",
   "rationale": "Some instruments cannot be machine-loaded at all, which is a hard blocker no orchestrator software can solve and a decisive reason an otherwise capable instrument cannot enter a workcell. This is why the cell-integration floor requires at least Partial here. Because a robot must approach the instrument and reach into it, the instrument's geometry is part of the integration contract: without a model of it, an integrator cannot lay out the workcell or plan collision-free trajectories. The criterion deliberately does not ask the instrument to carry labware identity or maintain the workflow-level history of labware movements: the orchestrator and the LIMS maintain that planned and historical state. Physical-presence observations, where the instrument can sense them, are exposed under C20 and are reconciled with the planned state by the orchestration layer. Physical labware presence, where the instrument can sense it, is reported as a readiness state under C20; linking results to samples is handled by execution identifiers under C5."
