   "summary": "Accurate, mutually consistent applicable access, motion and presence states, with a local robot-access indication where applicable",
   "applicability": null,
   "requirement": "The instrument SHOULD expose machine-readable, subscribable readiness states for the device-local conditions that affect external access: door, lid, or shutter status; internal-axis position and motion state (parked versus in motion); clamp, gripper, nest, or carrier engagement; labware presence at expected positions; and a vendor-declared robot-access state indicating that all internal moving parts are parked at positions the vendor has documented as safe for an external arm to enter. Applicability is per instrument class: an instrument with motorized axes exposes axis and motion state, one with retention or gripper mechanisms exposes engagement state, one with labware sensors exposes presence, and one that sits inside a robot's work envelope exposes the robot-access state; the vendor declares which categories apply, with a rationale the buyer accepts or rejects. Where none of the state categories apply to the instrument (no door, lid, or shutter; no motorized axes; no retention or gripper mechanism; no labware sensor; and not sited within a robot's work envelope), C20 is graded N/A and excluded from the applicable maximum. What matters is that these states are accurate and mutually consistent: when the instrument reports it is clear for access, its moving parts are in fact parked. No universal update-latency target is imposed, though a vendor MAY document typical latency. The interface SHOULD nevertheless document how long a readiness observation remains valid and how a subsequent change invalidates an earlier access-ready indication, since a state that is correct when sampled can be stale by the time another component acts on it. Global access coordination remains the responsibility of the workcell controller or orchestrator. These states are software coordination primitives, not safety-rated signals: they MUST NOT be used as personnel-safety inputs, and hardware safety events are covered by C21.",
