   "grading": "Pass requires all applicable state categories to be exposed, accurate, and mutually consistent, with the robot-access state tracking the underlying conditions; where the vendor has not declared applicability, the evaluator treats all categories as applicable and notes the missing declaration as a documentation gap. Partial covers only a subset of applicable categories exposed, or all categories exposed but the applicability declaration absent. Fail covers no machine-readable readiness state, or an inaccurate or self-contradictory one — in particular a robot-access state that reports \"clear\" while the underlying door, axes, or mechanisms are not in their arm-safe positions; a false \"clear\" is a coordination-integrity failure and is not treated as an incomplete-documentation Partial.",
   "test": "For each applicable state (door, lid, or shutter; internal-axis position and motion, including a documented blocked-movement condition; clamp, gripper, nest, or carrier engagement; and labware presence), drive or simulate the condition through its documented values and at least one transition, and confirm the API reports the matching value at each point, including intermediate states; on physical hardware, cross-check the reported value against the actual condition, observed directly or with an independent sensor. Then verify that the vendor-declared robot-access state changes consistently with those underlying states, so it reports \"clear\" only when the door, axes, and mechanisms are actually in their arm-safe positions.",
   "rationale": "When an arm reaches into an instrument, the workcell controller needs accurate device-local state about the instrument's configuration to plan and validate the arm's trajectory, and the instrument is the primary source of the device-local observations it can sense directly, which is why exposing them is a vendor responsibility. These observations are evidence the orchestrator reconciles with its planned labware and workflow state; they do not make the instrument authoritative for the global labware history, nor do they guarantee the reported observation is identical to physical truth. These are coordination primitives, not safety-rated signals: they support workcell-level safety validation but do not replace it, and an API state named \"safe to enter\" must not be wired into a safety function. Personnel safety and stop functions are handled at the workcell level and under C21. Neither SiLA 2 nor LADS standardizes this set of states."
