   "summary": "Where applicable, latched hardware-stop reporting and clearance behavior, plus timed orchestrator-issued operational shutdown",
   "applicability": "C21 applies to instruments that can create a hazard the workcell must be able to stop: those with active or hazardous actuation, hazardous energy output, robot interaction, or a stop function wired into the workcell. Typical in-scope instruments are liquid handlers, centrifuges, robotic arms, plate and tube handlers, automated storage and retrieval systems and instruments with high-pressure pumps, heaters, lasers, or other hazardous energy, especially when deployed inside a robotic workcell with a shared emergency-stop or safety bus. C21 is N/A in its entirety for instruments with none of these: no active actuation, no hazardous energy output, no robot interaction and no externally relevant stop function. Typical N/A examples are balances, barcode readers, passive cameras and non-actuated sensors (simple temperature probes, conductivity sensors, and similar). For such instruments, the relevant interface obligations are instead: expose communication-loss status through the normal status channel; qualify readings with a data-validity or staleness indicator; avoid returning unqualified stale measurements after a communication gap; and recover to a valid reporting state without requiring manual intervention after reconnect. These are data-quality obligations for passive instruments; they build on the control-loss observability of C15 and the observable-state discipline of C10, but go beyond what those criteria score for actuating instruments, so for a passive instrument the buyer SHOULD evaluate them explicitly (staleness qualification, suppression of unqualified stale readings, and recovery to valid reporting after a communication gap) rather than assume they are captured by the C15 and C10 scores. The N/A determination MUST be documented with the same vendor-verification path required for sub-test (a) N/A below: an electrical schematic or safety I/O declaration confirming the absence of active actuators and external safety-bus inputs, or integrator confirmation that the deployment includes no safety relay, E-stop bus, or PLC safety I/O connected to the instrument. A vendor assertion of N/A unsupported by any of these means should be treated as unverified and noted in the evaluation record.",
   "requirement": "The software interface MUST participate in hardware safety events without being treated as the safety function itself. The API signal is not safety-rated unless explicitly certified as part of the safety function; stop-category selection and hardware-safety-chain validation remain part of the workcell-level safety design. The prohibition targets misrepresentation of safety capability, not accurate reporting of an event. A non-rated status indication MAY name the hardware event it reports (for example, that a workcell emergency stop or protective stop has been asserted), because identifying which event occurred is part of accurate reporting. What vendor documentation, API schemas and property or method names MUST NOT do is present the software signal itself as a safety-rated function: labeling it \"safety-rated,\" as a Safe Torque Off (STO) or Safety Integrity Level (SIL) output, or otherwise implying it discharges the safety function, unless the signal is independently certified as part of the safety function under the applicable machinery safety standard (ISO 13849, IEC 62061, or equivalent). Representing a non-rated signal as a certified safety function is a criterion Fail regardless of functional behavior, because such labeling creates a safety architecture vulnerability by which workcell integrators may inadvertently wire a non-rated software signal into a safety function.\n\nTwo sub-tests apply. Sub-test (a) Hardware safety event reporting. When an external hardware safety event occurs (workcell-level emergency stop, safety bus signal, or other stop function as defined in IEC 60204-1), the device MUST NOT auto-resume without explicit clearance. If control power and communication remain available, the API MUST expose the latched safety-event status and the declared device state through the normal status channel. If the safety function interrupts communication or controller power, the endpoint MUST expose the latched event, last-known command state and recovery or clearance requirement on reconnect or restart before accepting normal commands; where the safety function interrupts controller power and the instrument cannot preserve the event locally, the vendor MUST document the restart-state behavior, what information is lost, what information is available from the workcell PLC or safety relay, and how the orchestrator must verify recovery before normal commands resume. This exception path can support at most Partial unless the workcell controller supplies the missing latched-state evidence.\n\nSub-test (a) does not apply to instruments without external safety-bus inputs by design (typically benchtop instruments such as pH meters, balances, and basic spectrophotometers); for such instruments, sub-test (a) is graded N/A and the criterion is graded against sub-test (b) only, with vendor documentation justifying the N/A determination. Because the N/A determination is otherwise entirely vendor self-reported, the evaluator SHOULD independently verify it by requesting the instrument's electrical schematic or safety I/O declaration showing the absence of external safety-bus inputs, or by confirming with the deployment integrator that no safety relay, E-stop bus, or PLC safety I/O is wired to the instrument in the intended deployment configuration; a vendor assertion of N/A unsupported by any of these means should be treated as unverified and noted in the evaluation record. Where the deployment configuration is not yet finalized at procurement time, the evaluator SHOULD record the N/A determination as provisional (\"N/A—pending deployment validation\") rather than final, and require re-evaluation of sub-test (a) as part of the deployment qualification before the instrument enters unmanned workcell operation; a vendor N/A claim accepted at procurement does not exempt the instrument from sub-test (a) evaluation at deployment qualification if the final wiring includes any safety relay, E-stop bus, or PLC safety I/O connected to the instrument.\n\nSub-test (b) Orchestrator-issued operational shutdown command. The API MUST accept an orchestrator-issued operational shutdown command and reach a documented operational shutdown state within a documented interval. This command is not safety-rated unless separately certified as part of the safety function.",
