   "grading": "Criterion-level N/A applies when the instrument meets the passive-instrument definition in the applicability note above; such instruments are excluded from the applicable maximum score and their interface obligations are evaluated under C15 and C10 instead. For in-scope instruments, the sub-test grading rule applies: Pass requires every applicable sub-test to pass; Partial when at least one applicable sub-test passes; Fail when none. For instruments where sub-test (a) is graded N/A (per the requirement), the criterion is graded against sub-test (b) only. Where sub-test (a) is applicable and is evaluated via fallback evidence rather than full hardware signal assertion in the deployment configuration (per the test above), the criterion is capped at Partial regardless of the sub-test (b) outcome, and full hardware assertion of sub-test (a) is then required for a Pass-grade criterion score. Where sub-test (a) is graded N/A, the criterion is graded on sub-test (b) alone and this hardware-assertion cap does not apply. The evaluation order is: criterion-level N/A first, then sub-test (a) applicability, then sub-test grading and evidence caps.",
   "test": "For (a), assert the configured hardware safety signal during a long-running command and verify the instrument reaches its declared safe state. Where asserting the hardware safety signal pre-purchase is not feasible (workcell-level E-stop bus unavailable, safety relay not present, or the instrument is evaluated outside its intended deployment workcell), the evaluator SHOULD use one of the following fallback paths and document which was used: a test fixture simulating the safety-bus input signal per the vendor's electrical schematic; simulator-based evidence from the vendor demonstrating the latched-event and clearance-requirement behavior; factory-acceptance records covering the safety-event path; or reference-customer testimony from a documented production deployment. Fallback paths other than full hardware signal assertion correspond to at most Partial on sub-test (a), because the software behavior cannot be confirmed without asserting the actual signal in the deployment configuration. For (b), issue the orchestrator-issued operational shutdown command during a long-running command and verify that the instrument reaches its declared operational shutdown state. In both cases, verify that issuing a normal command after the safety event is rejected until clearance is given.",
   "rationale": "When a workcell-level stop or safety event occurs, every affected instrument must enter its designed response state and, where communication remains available or after controller restart, expose the latched event and recovery requirement before normal commands resume. The interface signal is a non-safety-rated operational fail-safe, not a substitute for the workcell's safety-rated infrastructure; an orchestrator-issued operational shutdown command is a distinct mechanism for graceful coordinated shutdown. Control-loss observability and safe-state support (C15) addresses control-client failure; this criterion addresses workcell-wide coordinated shutdown; C20 addresses automation-readiness states that inform automation coordination but that the workcell's independently enforced safety function does not rely on. The three criteria sit in a layered relationship: C15 backstops a single control session, safety-event participation backstops a coordinated workcell shutdown and access readiness provides the device-local inputs the workcell's automation layer composes into trajectory decisions, kept separate from the independently enforced personnel-safety functions."
